{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md collab/1792263000000-reviewer-admission-in-linker-r2.prompt.md collab/1792261000000-reviewer-admission-in-linker.findings.md collab/1792262000000-architect-admission-in-linker-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-12 14:30:00 GMT\nCoding-Agent: codex (gpt-6.1-sol)\n# Task: re-review the round-2 admission-in-the-linker design (read-only, architectural)\nYour round 1 verdict was REJECT (four P1 + one P2): collab/1792261000000-reviewer-admission-in-linker.findings.md. Round-2 design (fable): collab/1792262000000-architect-admission-in-linker-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md; its brief with the owner's correction (the linker is language-blind; the :yin.link/importer key is deleted): collab/1792262000000-architect-admission-in-linker-r2.prompt.md. SCOPE CONTRACT: block only on an in-scope P0/P1 (an unsound guard, a source-contradicted claim, a slice that cannot meet its acceptance) or a P2 in newly introduced behavior; the rest non-blocking with owner. For each of your four P1s and the P2 state FIXED / NOT FIXED / PARTIAL with evidence in the design AND the source it relies on (src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc, admission.cljc, repl.cljc incl. ~1371/1398/1956 and drive-links, yin.vm.linker, yin.vm.module ~552, engine ~1289-1307/1396-1403, the tests named in the consumer inventory). Then check: (1) the language-blindness is complete: no language/frontend/[id revision] remains in any datum, slice, acceptance or the context; the holder context is composition-owned; (2) the exposure statement matches the source (installation family, `:admission` record, registry hits ask nothing; \"untrust is not retroactive\"); (3) the tick-to-VM handoff (the tick retains the round's successor and passes its progress into the pending driver; `drive-links` gains an initial-progress argument; one hook replaces the two recheck hooks) closes the late-allow gap you found, with no double-run, no lost successor on a raising VM run, bounded work, and the acceptance (a)-(d) would fail on regression; (4) AL-1 as a pure relocation really preserves the API and topology (every public var, the `composition`/`serve` names, the session topology), the consumer inventory is complete (grep it yourself), the dependency check replacing the literal grep works, Dart stale-output handling; (5) the Q4 pin-check placement and its corrected explanation (declared-obligation / `declared-obligation` ~2088, Python import via quoted require): sound; (6) AD-2 compatibility and the fate table; AL-3/AL-4 as re-sliced; (7) invariants (A only identity; dao.stream P2P; fetch has no deadline; no capability vocabulary in the linker; register-VM cutover independence; ruling 19 trust vs safety). Do not edit or run suites. Output: verdict (APPROVE / APPROVE-WITH-NITS / REJECT) first; if none write \"No open in-scope P0/P1\"; numbered findings with file:line, severity, exact fix. FINAL MESSAGE must contain the complete review beginning with Completed-GMT / Completed-Local lines.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-12 12:35:01 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 19:35:01 +07 (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh)\n\n**REJECT**\n\nThe relocation is architecturally appropriate, but the design contains source-contradicted layering and exposure claims, and leaves the automatic-resume handoff underspecified. Its AL-1 acceptance also cannot hold with the stated edit boundary.\n\n1. **P1 — The content-driver inversion claim is false.**  \n   Design: `collab/1792260000000-architect-admission-in-linker.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:6,25`. Source: [driver.clj:20](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj:20).\n\n   `dao.jing.content.driver` requires only `dao.jing`, `dao.jing.content.step`, and `dao.stream`. Its docstring identifies `yin.repl.link` as a downstream consumer through the coordinate opener; that is the opposite dependency direction. It requires neither REPL namespace to link.\n\n   The other two facts are true: [dht_import_test.cljc:46](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/python/antlr/dht_import_test.cljc:46) requires both namespaces, and [linker_require_test.cljc:34](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:34) requires `yin.repl.link`.\n\n   **Exact fix:** Remove the driver from the inversion evidence and re-require list; update its consumer docstring after relocation. Retain the move’s justification from the two verified consumers and the actual request-serving semantics. **Owner:** architect.\n\n2. **P1 — “Never crosses a task boundary” contradicts registry inheritance; changing importer on `(lang ...)` does not create isolation.**  \n   Design: findings file `:47–49`. Sources: [module.cljc:552](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:552), [engine.cljc:1289](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1289), [engine.cljc:1396](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1396), [repl.cljc:1872](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1872).\n\n   Install children inherit admitted entries, and dependencies installed by children transfer to parents. Authorization therefore already crosses task boundaries within an installation family. Today `(lang ...)` changes only `:lang`, preserving the VM and registry.\n\n   With AL-3’s proposed restamping, Clojure can admit M, then Python in the same task can obtain M through the registry hit without consulting a verifier that refuses Python. Calling the session “legacy” does not resolve the contradiction between a lifetime importer and an importer changed at language selection. Ruling 18 prohibits merging runtime state across frontend revisions; it does not establish that today’s REPL allocates isolated tasks.\n\n   **Exact fix:** State that admission follows the registry and its installation-family inheritance. Choose one explicit AL-3 rule:\n   \n   - Keep importer immutable for that family, document mixed-language registry reuse, and defer frontend isolation to the profile-task slice; or\n   - Make language/profile selection choose a separate task and registry before claiming frontend isolation.\n\n   Add a same-session Clojure → Python acceptance case, alongside the two-session case. Retain cache hits without questions only within the chosen exposure domain. **Owner:** architect and frontend/VM engineer.\n\n3. **P1 — `round` progress alone does not specify a safe tick-to-VM handoff.**  \n   Design: findings file `:37,60,87`. Source: [repl.cljc:1371](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1371), [repl.cljc:1398](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1398), [repl.cljc:1956](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1956).\n\n   The proposed hook runs a `round`, then rechecks when that round reports progress. A late allow can make that first round append the link response and advance the request cursor. A subsequent `recheck-pending` calls `drive-links`; its own round now finds no work and reports no progress. The existing driver consequently never calls `vm/run` to consume the already-appended response.\n\n   The consuming drain eliminates answer-window starvation, but its progress flag must reach the waiting VM. The stated protocol does not establish that handoff.\n\n   **Exact fix:** Specify that the tick retains the round successor and passes its response/progress into the pending driver, which runs the VM once before requiring another interpreter round to report progress. Alternatively, make the pending driver perform the tick’s round itself. Preserve one bounded drain per intended round and successor state before any raising VM run.\n\n   **Acceptance:** External allow arrives after the initial drive stops; the tick’s round delivers the response; no further question, input line, load event, or answer is needed to complete. Also exercise a decision snapshot becoming available after parking. **Owner:** linker/REPL engineer, AL-2.\n\n4. **P1 — AL-1’s edit boundary and acceptance are incompatible.**  \n   Design: findings file `:25,86`.\n\n   AL-1 deletes the old namespaces and renames `composition` to `source`, introduces `make`/`round`, and changes topology, while limiting REPL changes to requires. Existing REPL calls and state construction still use `composition`, `serve`, and separately minted admission state. A requires-only edit cannot use the proposed final interface.\n\n   The consumer inventory also omits [derive_test.cljc:12](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:12). The move/rename affects the path-and-function allowlist in [store_write_audit_test.clj:331](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:331).\n\n   The literal empty grep cannot pass: legitimate matches include the publication datum `:yin.repl/root`, transport error keywords, docstrings, and `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md`.\n\n   **Exact fix:** Make AL-1 a namespace relocation preserving callable API and session topology; put API/topology reduction in AL-2. Alternatively, include the necessary REPL adaptation in AL-1 explicitly. Inventory all require forms, update the store-write allowlist, and replace the literal grep with a namespace-dependency check that excludes documentation and datum vocabulary. Regenerate renamed Dart namespaces and remove stale outputs before relying on a glob-based run. **Owner:** linker engineer.\n\n5. **P2, non-blocking — Narrow the AL-2 lexical assertion and state the complete gate ladder.**  \n   Design: findings file `:23,79–89`. Sources: [repl.cljc:944](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:944), [build-n-test.md:126](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/build-n-test.md:126).\n\n   Keeping `create-state` admission configuration necessarily permits executable occurrences of `admission` in `repl.cljc`; AL-4 also edits `:link-source :admission :trusted`. “Only option docstrings and pending-text” is therefore inconsistent with the retained configuration role.\n\n   `bb test:sub yin.vm yin.repl yang.python` is a checkpoint selection, not a replacement for changed-dependent, master, and slow phase-boundary gates.\n\n   **Exact fix:** Assert absence of REPL-owned admission protocol state, drain/judge/serve calls, and admission-specific hooks; allow configuration plumbing and display text. Explicitly retain G0–G4, with quick gates per landing and slow gates at phase boundaries. Add `dao.jing` selection if its files are actually edited. **Owner:** architect/test owner.\n\nThe remaining requested rulings follow from source inspection:\n\n- **Layering:** Moving the request interpreter, resolution/admission binding, delivery, and retirement into `yin.vm.linker.interpreter`, with the protocol in `yin.vm.linker.admission`, is sound. No dependency cycle is inherent in that move: follow/authority/configuration must remain supplied data, and neither new namespace should require `yin.repl.dht`. Shell tokens, pending text, and typed-line handling stay outside. The tables are functionally coherent once findings 1 and 4 are corrected.\n\n- **Earlier P1 mapping:** Retained-resolution binding remains enforced by `admitted` at [link.cljc:399](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:399). Retirement remains enforced by normalized half-open intervals and delimiter-qualified descendants at [link.cljc:587](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:587). Starvation prevention remains the persisted consuming drain at [admission.cljc:110](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/admission.cljc:110), with full `[link-id serial]` correlation at line 95. Automatic late resume needs finding 3’s handoff. The earlier boot-trust P1 stays enforced by explicit trust independent of declared authorities. Rollback must carry the entire new interpreter value before VM execution can raise.\n\n- **Importer envelope:** The optional key is compatible with the pair shape, section 9 response correlation, and engine waits. It requires both closed-key-set and portability-validator edits at [linker.cljc:1486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:1486): permit exactly the intended two-element portable vector, not arbitrary collections. All four kernels’ constructors and `spawn-module` implementations must preserve it; adding an engine child option alone is insufficient because kernels select options explicitly. Test malformed vectors, host/function elements, omission, and child propagation.\n\n- **Q4:** The gap and engine placement are sound. The wording is slightly inaccurate: [linker.cljc:2088](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:2088) recognizes a retained free name equal to the required module itself **or** qualified by it. Python’s imported dependency is supplied through a quoted require and module-object operations, rather than such a retained free name; Python still has other qualified names such as `py/import`. Check every manifest require against the halted child’s linked entry before lifting/publishing the installed slice; absent or unequal entries refuse. This is admission-independent and backend-neutral. Its acceptance should bypass the DHT early guard and cover inherited and freshly installed mismatches on all four VMs.\n\n- **AD-1 / AD-2:** AD-2 first is architecturally safe when it removes the hook, option, implementation, documentation, and Python-specific assertions together, retaining the uniform admission seam and safety prerequisites. Current source still contains the hook; the available AD-2 log reports waiting for Node, so its completion is unverified. Keep AD-1’s validated allow shapes, registry records, binding, drain, retirement, and rollback. Move their ownership as proposed. AL-4 can remain configuration-only; rebuilding policy must preserve protocol cursors, retained questions, serials, and external mode. Untrust remains nonretroactive.\n\n- **Invariants:** The redesign introduces no second code identity, privileged P2P node, capability-token semantics in the linker, or fetch deadline. Importer identity is policy input, not code identity. Allow must continue to leave profile, pin, and export-origin checks intact. The AST-level protocol is independent of the register-VM cutover, although importer propagation and Q4 acceptance must cover every backend.\n\nRead-only review completed. No files edited or suites run. The requested AD-1 round-2 findings file is absent; its complete review was available in the corresponding stdout log.\nPlan file saved. Here is the complete revised design.\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-12 14:21 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 21:21 +07 (Asia/Bangkok)\n\n**Verdict.** The relocation stands; the importer idea is withdrawn. Codex's four P1s were all correct and are fixed below. The content driver is dropped from the evidence, the exposure rule follows the registry and its installation family, the tick now hands its round's successor and progress to the pending driver, and AL-1 becomes a pure relocation with the API reduction in AL-2. The owner's correction supersedes section 3: there is no language at the linker level.\n\n## 0. Owner correction, applied\n\nAll languages compile to the universal AST. yin.vm does not know the source language and the linker must not either. No linker datum carries a language, a frontend or an `[id revision]`: `:yin.link/importer` and `:yin.admit/importer` are deleted from this design, the closed request key set is not edited, and no kernel constructor or `spawn-module` changes. Admission is about principals (signing keys that asserted a name), addresses (A, the only code identity) and names. \"Who is asking\" is the holder: a composition-owned context the interpreter receives as the `context` argument of `(verify decision context question)`, today built by `admission/context` from the session's own key. A published module is AST at an address; any frontend may import it when the holder's policy admits that principal for that address. Registry reuse between frontends is not a linker security question. Frontend runtime-state isolation (ruling 18, Python's `py.rt` cells) is a frontend and session concern outside the linker and is not designed here.\n\n## 1. Layering\n\nRule: linker semantics is what a plain Clojure consumer (dht.md section 10) must have to resolve, ask, bind, deliver, discharge or retire a link. REPL is what disappears with the REPL: cadence, display, retained lines, which principals this session names.\n\nEvidence, verified: `test/yang/python/antlr/dht_import_test.cljc:46-47` requires both REPL namespaces to run a Python DHT import; `test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:34` and `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:12` require `yin.repl.link` to mint a pair and serve a VM-level request. The request-serving semantics (resolve, ask, bind, deliver, retire) exists only in `yin.repl.link/serve`. Correction: `dao.jing.content.driver` requires only dao.jing, dao.jing.content.step and dao.stream (`src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj:20`); its docstring names yin.repl.link as a downstream consumer. It is not evidence and is not re-required; its docstring is updated in AL-1.\n\n| Lives in | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` (from `yin.repl.link`) | pair minting, `composition`, `authority`, `serve`, `run-descriptor`, `retire`, `formats`, constants; after AL-2 also `make`, `round`, `reconfigure` |\n| `yin.vm.linker.admission` (from `yin.repl.admission`) | protocol unchanged: `make-state`, `drain`, `ask`, `asked`, `answer-to`, `judge`, `settle`, `forget`, `context`, `trusted-asserter`, `policy`, `interpreter`, `serve`, `reasons` |\n| `yin.vm.engine` / `yin.vm.module` | the `:admission` registry record as landed; the pinned-dependency check (section 5) |\n| `yin.repl` | configuration (`:link-source`, `:admission` option, `--dht-trust`, `:external`), one `:linker` value after AL-2, one tick hook, trust commands, display text |\n\nNeither new namespace requires any `yin.repl*` namespace; follow, authority and configuration stay supplied data. Old namespaces deleted, no shim.\n\n## 2. The linker's admission boundary\n\nDatums: question and answer shapes of 7.5.1 verbatim, no added key. Allow key set closed at id, decision, conditions; a refusal keeps reason and open keys.\n\nMinting (AL-2): `interpreter/make` mints the link pair and, only for a `:dht?` source, the admission pair and answer cursor. A local or remote content source has no admission pair.\n\nWhere the wait lives: a parked link is a request the interpreter's request cursor has not passed plus a retained question under `:asked` (`link.cljc:399` and `:427`). Nothing lands on the response medium until the answer does, so the VM's wait entry never learns admission exists. No new state.\n\nOne round, `(interpreter/round linker node budget)`, in order: drain answers (one bounded drain per round, the `admission/drain` semantics), serve link requests stopping at the first pending, serve the in-process interpreter when the value holds one. Returns the successor value, the node, the pending entries and a progress flag. Progress is any of: a response appended, a question appended, an in-process answer appended, a drained answer matched a retained question. The successor is always carried, even without progress.\n\nAttachment: in process, `:admission` builds the policy (`trusted-asserter` over own plus `:trusted`, or a composed `:verify` over a static `:decision` or a capacity-1 `:decision-medium`). External, `:admission :external` mints the pair and runs no interpreter; another process reads questions and appends answers over any dao.stream exposure, no privileged node. ShiBi attaches as either with no linker edit.\n\nAuthority versus admission: declared principals (R8) decide which assertions count in the fold; trusted principals decide which resolved entries the holder accepts. Independent inputs. A `:composition` provenance entry is never asked.\n\nDischarge and L-d: ask after resolve, before load; discharge is step 5b after delivery, untouched. An admission refusal is a section 9 link refusal, so try-require catches it as it catches `:absent`. An install child's transitive requirement is asked on its own request.\n\n**Exposure, stated exactly.** Admission governs entry into a registry. An allow admits one address into the requesting task's registry, and authorization follows that registry and its installation-family inheritance: `link-install` spawns the child over the parent's linked entries and receives each into it (`engine.cljc` ~1289-1307); the child's installs transfer back to the parent with the `:admission` record (~1396-1403); a name already resolvable in the registry answers with no link request and hence no question (`module.cljc` ~552-554). The exposure domain is the installation family for its life, not one task. It is about principal plus address plus holder context, and it is language-blind: any frontend compiling to a require of that name hits the same entry. \"Untrust is not retroactive\" means `(untrust p)` changes the decision snapshot for questions not yet answered; it evicts no registry entry, revokes no appended response, and leaves a question already answered allow allowed. `(reset)` and `(vm ...)` start a fresh registry and fresh admission state.\n\n## 3. The tick-to-VM handoff\n\nChosen: the tick retains the round's successor and passes its progress into the pending driver, which runs the VM once before requiring another round to report progress. One hook replaces `recheck-on-load-events` and `recheck-on-answers`: `(recheck-on-tick state events)`.\n\n1. Run one `round` on the session's `:linker` with the session node; assoc the successor and node into state before anything else, so a raising VM run carries it (`link-raise` keeps the one `::linker` key, `carry-served` restores it).\n2. If the round reported progress, call the pending driver with progress carried in. `drive-links` gains an initial-progress argument: its first iteration runs the VM once without a round of its own (no second drain before that run), then continues the existing loop, bounded by `link-round-budget`.\n3. Else, if the events hold a loaded or load-failed event for a manifest a pending link waits on, or an installed head of an awaited principal, recheck as today. The no-progress `:checks` counter and the link policy are unchanged.\n4. Else nothing happens.\n\nAcceptance (AL-2, three hosts): (a) a require parks on an external question and the initial drive stops; an allow is appended; one tick's round delivers the response and the run completes with no further question, input line, load event or answer. (b) The same with an empty decision medium at park time and a snapshot appended after parking: the in-process interpreter answers in one tick's round, the drain matches and the response lands inside the driver's loop, and the run completes. (c) A refusal arriving the same way raises as the VM's own with the successor `:linker` carried into the rolled-back state. (d) A tick with no progress and no relevant event rechecks nothing and leaves `:checks` unchanged.\n\n## 4. What to do with what landed\n\n| AD-1 piece | Ruling |\n|---|---|\n| `yin.repl.admission` | MOVE to `yin.vm.linker.admission` (AL-1); `context` keeps `:yin.admit/session` through AL-1, AL-3 replaces it |\n| `yin.repl.link` | MOVE to `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` (AL-1), API preserved |\n| admission pair in `make-session` | untouched in AL-1; AL-2 moves it into `make`, dht-only |\n| `serve-admission`, `admission-context`, `:admitter`, `recheck-on-answers` | untouched in AL-1; AL-2 deletes them |\n| `link-raise` / `carry-served` | AL-2: carry one `::linker` key |\n| `abandon-pending` | AL-2: one `retire` call |\n| `--dht-trust`, `:admission`, `:external` | KEEP, configuration |\n| engine `:admission` registry record | KEEP |\n| `admission_test`, `admission_doc_test` | MOVE to test/yin/vm/linker/ (AL-1) |\n\nAD-2 (delete Python `principal-admission` and the `:admit` hook; `publication.cljc:130` still present) is compatible and lands first; AL-1 rebases over it. AD-3 becomes AL-4.\n\n## 5. Trust versus safety; the pinned-dependency fix\n\nUnchanged: trust is the interpreter's answer, safety is the linker's and L-e's, an allow never relaxes L-e, conditions stay empty.\n\nQ4, wording corrected: `declared-obligation` (`linker.cljc` ~2088) recognizes a retained free name equal to the required module or qualified by it. Python's imported dependency arrives through a quoted require and module-object operations, not such a retained name, so no module obligation exists and discharge compares nothing; the DHT source's dependency-bindings check is the only guard. Fix, engine seat, lands with L-d, independent of admission: in the `linked` transition before lifting or publishing the installed slice, check every pinned require of the manifest against the halted child's linked entry for that name; absent or unequal refuses `:dependency-pin-mismatch`. Acceptance bypasses the DHT early guard and covers inherited and freshly installed mismatches on all four VMs. AL-0 corrects the false 7.4 sentence.\n\n## 6. Slices, inventory, gates\n\nConsumer inventory (require forms, verified by grep, excluding test/cljd-out): `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:19,23`; `src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:40`; `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc:63`; `test/yang/python/antlr/dht_import_test.cljc:46-47`; `test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:20`; `test/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:23`; `test/yin/repl/admission_test.cljc:25,27`; `test/yin/repl/admission_doc_test.cljc:11-12`; `test/yin/repl/serve_test.cljc:22`; `test/yin/repl/python_antlr_test.cljc:31`; `test/yin/repl/require_test.cljc:28`; `test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:34`; `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:12`. Docstring mentions (publication.cljc:132, driver.clj:7, repl.cljc, store_write_audit_test.clj:368, linker_require_test.cljc:1323) are text edits.\n\nGate ladder (docs/agents/build-n-test.md): G0 iterate `bb test:sub:clj <own>`; G1 checkpoint `bb test:sub yin.vm yin.repl yang.python`, adding `dao.jing` when a dao.jing file is edited (AL-1's driver docstring is one); G2 land `bb test:changed` on three lanes plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` where slow tests exist; G3 master `bb test` after merge; G4 slow tier at phase boundaries, after AL-2 and after AL-4. Before any Dart lane after a rename, `rm -rf test/cljd-out` so the aggregator does not pick up stale generated files.\n\n- **AL-0 docs.** dht.md 7.5 rewritten as the linker's admission with section 0's ruling, 7.5.7 replaced by section 2's exposure text, 7.4 corrected; linker.md new 6.5 and section 10 updated; dao.shibi.md first-consumer paragraph names the linker; yang.antlr.md ~3044 pointer; docs/agents/architecture.md gains the layering rule; src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md pointer. Acceptance: `admission-doc-test` green on three hosts against the rewritten section.\n- **AL-1 relocation** (linker seat). `git mv` both namespaces and both admission tests; every public var and the session topology preserved (`composition` and `serve` keep their names; `:link-pair`, `:admission`, `:admitter`, `serve-admission`, `recheck-on-answers` untouched); all require forms updated; store-write allowlist key moved to `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc` with the same `composition` entry; driver docstring updated. Acceptance: admission tests, `require_test`, `dht_test`, `linker_require_test`, `derive_test` green on three hosts; the namespace-dependency check is empty:\n\n```\ngrep -rn -E '^\\s*\\[yin\\.repl(\\.[a-z-]+)? ' src/cljc/yin/vm src/cljc/yang src/clj/dao --include='*.clj*'\n```\n\n  It matches require vectors only, so docs and datum vocabulary such as `:yin.repl/root` and error keywords do not match. `bb test:sub:list yin.vm` shows the two new test namespaces; slow `dht_import_test` green on the JVM. Collab note to the yang.python orchestrator before touching its tests.\n- **AL-2 REPL reduction and handoff** (REPL seat, with the linker-side `make` and `round` in the same slice). Acceptance: section 3 (a) to (d); F3, P1-2 (i) and (iii), P2-3 against `round`; lexical: `repl.cljc` requires `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` and not `yin.vm.linker.admission`; no session keys `:admission` or `:admitter`; no calls to `drain`, `judge`, `ask`, `settle` or the admission `serve`; exactly one tick hook; configuration plumbing and display text allowed.\n- **AL-3 holder context** (linker seat). `composition` gains an optional `:holder` (a principal id, or a token the verify understands); `context` answers the subject from it, defaulting to the own key's principal; `:yin.admit/session` deleted; `reconfigure` replaces the policy of a `:linker` value while preserving the interpreter's cursors, retained questions, serials and external mode. Acceptance, three hosts: a recording verify receives the subject the composition supplied; reconfiguring to a new trusted set answers a still-parked question under the new decision with cursors and serials unchanged.\n- **AL-4 trust commands** (REPL seat). `(trust p)`, `(untrust p)`, `(trusted)` edit the `:trusted` configuration and call `reconfigure`. Acceptance: AD-3's list through `round`, plus untrust-not-retroactive from section 2.\n\nOrder: AD-2, AL-0, AL-1, AL-2, AL-3, AL-4. Slow tier after AL-2 and AL-4.\n\nMust not land before L-e: any claim an untrusted-origin import is safe; a default decision beyond own principals; honoring a non-empty condition; weakening profile, pin or export-origin checks; admission in a frontend or on the content pair. The design is AST-level data on streams; the register-VM cutover does not touch it.\n\n**Unverified.** AD-2 lane results (source still has the hook). That `bb test:sub` maps the relocated tests into `yin.vm` by prefix (subsystems.edn suggests yes). That `yin.repl.dht-process-test` needs no edit beyond requires. The Python no-retained-name claim comes from codex and I1, not a run. Nothing was run or edited; the plan file at `/Users/sto/.claude/plans/created-gmt-2026-10-12-14-00-00-gmt-curried-wreath.md` holds this same text.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1792261000000-reviewer-admission-in-linker.findings.md; rg -n 'yin\\\\.repl\\\\.(link|admission)|defn |def |drive-links|recheck-on|link-raise|carry-served|serve-admission|admission-context' src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/admission.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc test src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1792261000000-reviewer-admission-in-linker.findings.md; rg -n 'yin\\\\.repl\\\\.(link|admission)|defn |def |drive-links|recheck-on|link-raise|carry-served|serve-admission|admission-context' src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/admission.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc test src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-12 12:35:01 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 19:35:01 +07 (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh)\n\n**REJECT**\n\nThe relocation is architecturally appropriate, but the design contains source-contradicted layering and exposure claims, and leaves the automatic-resume handoff underspecified. Its AL-1 acceptance also cannot hold with the stated edit boundary.\n\n1. **P1 — The content-driver inversion claim is false.**  \n   Design: `collab/1792260000000-architect-admission-in-linker.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:6,25`. Source: [driver.clj:20](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj:20).\n\n   `dao.jing.content.driver` requires only `dao.jing`, `dao.jing.content.step`, and `dao.stream`. Its docstring identifies `yin.repl.link` as a downstream consumer through the coordinate opener; that is the opposite dependency direction. It requires neither REPL namespace to link.\n\n   The other two facts are true: [dht_import_test.cljc:46](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/python/antlr/dht_import_test.cljc:46) requires both namespaces, and [linker_require_test.cljc:34](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:34) requires `yin.repl.link`.\n\n   **Exact fix:** Remove the driver from the inversion evidence and re-require list; update its consumer docstring after relocation. Retain the move’s justification from the two verified consumers and the actual request-serving semantics. **Owner:** architect.\n\n2. **P1 — “Never crosses a task boundary” contradicts registry inheritance; changing importer on `(lang ...)` does not create isolation.**  \n   Design: findings file `:47–49`. Sources: [module.cljc:552](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:552), [engine.cljc:1289](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1289), [engine.cljc:1396](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1396), [repl.cljc:1872](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1872).\n\n   Install children inherit admitted entries, and dependencies installed by children transfer to parents. Authorization therefore already crosses task boundaries within an installation family. Today `(lang ...)` changes only `:lang`, preserving the VM and registry.\n\n   With AL-3’s proposed restamping, Clojure can admit M, then Python in the same task can obtain M through the registry hit without consulting a verifier that refuses Python. Calling the session “legacy” does not resolve the contradiction between a lifetime importer and an importer changed at language selection. Ruling 18 prohibits merging runtime state across frontend revisions; it does not establish that today’s REPL allocates isolated tasks.\n\n   **Exact fix:** State that admission follows the registry and its installation-family inheritance. Choose one explicit AL-3 rule:\n   \n   - Keep importer immutable for that family, document mixed-language registry reuse, and defer frontend isolation to the profile-task slice; or\n   - Make language/profile selection choose a separate task and registry before claiming frontend isolation.\n\n   Add a same-session Clojure → Python acceptance case, alongside the two-session case. Retain cache hits without questions only within the chosen exposure domain. **Owner:** architect and frontend/VM engineer.\n\n3. **P1 — `round` progress alone does not specify a safe tick-to-VM handoff.**  \n   Design: findings file `:37,60,87`. Source: [repl.cljc:1371](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1371), [repl.cljc:1398](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1398), [repl.cljc:1956](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1956).\n\n   The proposed hook runs a `round`, then rechecks when that round reports progress. A late allow can make that first round append the link response and advance the request cursor. A subsequent `recheck-pending` calls `drive-links`; its own round now finds no work and reports no progress. The existing driver consequently never calls `vm/run` to consume the already-appended response.\n\n   The consuming drain eliminates answer-window starvation, but its progress flag must reach the waiting VM. The stated protocol does not establish that handoff.\n\n   **Exact fix:** Specify that the tick retains the round successor and passes its response/progress into the pending driver, which runs the VM once before requiring another interpreter round to report progress. Alternatively, make the pending driver perform the tick’s round itself. Preserve one bounded drain per intended round and successor state before any raising VM run.\n\n   **Acceptance:** External allow arrives after the initial drive stops; the tick’s round delivers the response; no further question, input line, load event, or answer is needed to complete. Also exercise a decision snapshot becoming available after parking. **Owner:** linker/REPL engineer, AL-2.\n\n4. **P1 — AL-1’s edit boundary and acceptance are incompatible.**  \n   Design: findings file `:25,86`.\n\n   AL-1 deletes the old namespaces and renames `composition` to `source`, introduces `make`/`round`, and changes topology, while limiting REPL changes to requires. Existing REPL calls and state construction still use `composition`, `serve`, and separately minted admission state. A requires-only edit cannot use the proposed final interface.\n\n   The consumer inventory also omits [derive_test.cljc:12](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:12). The move/rename affects the path-and-function allowlist in [store_write_audit_test.clj:331](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:331).\n\n   The literal empty grep cannot pass: legitimate matches include the publication datum `:yin.repl/root`, transport error keywords, docstrings, and `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md`.\n\n   **Exact fix:** Make AL-1 a namespace relocation preserving callable API and session topology; put API/topology reduction in AL-2. Alternatively, include the necessary REPL adaptation in AL-1 explicitly. Inventory all require forms, update the store-write allowlist, and replace the literal grep with a namespace-dependency check that excludes documentation and datum vocabulary. Regenerate renamed Dart namespaces and remove stale outputs before relying on a glob-based run. **Owner:** linker engineer.\n\n5. **P2, non-blocking — Narrow the AL-2 lexical assertion and state the complete gate ladder.**  \n   Design: findings file `:23,79–89`. Sources: [repl.cljc:944](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:944), [build-n-test.md:126](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/build-n-test.md:126).\n\n   Keeping `create-state` admission configuration necessarily permits executable occurrences of `admission` in `repl.cljc`; AL-4 also edits `:link-source :admission :trusted`. “Only option docstrings and pending-text” is therefore inconsistent with the retained configuration role.\n\n   `bb test:sub yin.vm yin.repl yang.python` is a checkpoint selection, not a replacement for changed-dependent, master, and slow phase-boundary gates.\n\n   **Exact fix:** Assert absence of REPL-owned admission protocol state, drain/judge/serve calls, and admission-specific hooks; allow configuration plumbing and display text. Explicitly retain G0–G4, with quick gates per landing and slow gates at phase boundaries. Add `dao.jing` selection if its files are actually edited. **Owner:** architect/test owner.\n\nThe remaining requested rulings follow from source inspection:\n\n- **Layering:** Moving the request interpreter, resolution/admission binding, delivery, and retirement into `yin.vm.linker.interpreter`, with the protocol in `yin.vm.linker.admission`, is sound. No dependency cycle is inherent in that move: follow/authority/configuration must remain supplied data, and neither new namespace should require `yin.repl.dht`. Shell tokens, pending text, and typed-line handling stay outside. The tables are functionally coherent once findings 1 and 4 are corrected.\n\n- **Earlier P1 mapping:** Retained-resolution binding remains enforced by `admitted` at [link.cljc:399](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:399). Retirement remains enforced by normalized half-open intervals and delimiter-qualified descendants at [link.cljc:587](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc:587). Starvation prevention remains the persisted consuming drain at [admission.cljc:110](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/admission.cljc:110), with full `[link-id serial]` correlation at line 95. Automatic late resume needs finding 3’s handoff. The earlier boot-trust P1 stays enforced by explicit trust independent of declared authorities. Rollback must carry the entire new interpreter value before VM execution can raise.\n\n- **Importer envelope:** The optional key is compatible with the pair shape, section 9 response correlation, and engine waits. It requires both closed-key-set and portability-validator edits at [linker.cljc:1486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:1486): permit exactly the intended two-element portable vector, not arbitrary collections. All four kernels’ constructors and `spawn-module` implementations must preserve it; adding an engine child option alone is insufficient because kernels select options explicitly. Test malformed vectors, host/function elements, omission, and child propagation.\n\n- **Q4:** The gap and engine placement are sound. The wording is slightly inaccurate: [linker.cljc:2088](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:2088) recognizes a retained free name equal to the required module itself **or** qualified by it. Python’s imported dependency is supplied through a quoted require and module-object operations, rather than such a retained free name; Python still has other qualified names such as `py/import`. Check every manifest require against the halted child’s linked entry before lifting/publishing the installed slice; absent or unequal entries refuse. This is admission-independent and backend-neutral. Its acceptance should bypass the DHT early guard and cover inherited and freshly installed mismatches on all four VMs.\n\n- **AD-1 / AD-2:** AD-2 first is architecturally safe when it removes the hook, option, implementation, documentation, and Python-specific assertions together, retaining the uniform admission seam and safety prerequisites. Current source still contains the hook; the available AD-2 log reports waiting for Node, so its completion is unverified. Keep AD-1’s validated allow shapes, registry records, binding, drain, retirement, and rollback. Move their ownership as proposed. AL-4 can remain configuration-only; rebuilding policy must preserve protocol cursors, retained questions, serials, and external mode. Untrust remains nonretroactive.\n\n- **Invariants:** The redesign introduces no second code identity, privileged P2P node, capability-token semantics in the linker, or fetch deadline. Importer identity is policy input, not code identity. Allow must continue to leave profile, pin, and export-origin checks intact. The AST-level protocol is independent of the register-VM cutover, although importer propagation and Q4 acceptance must cover every backend.\n\nRead-only review completed. No files edited or suites run. The requested AD-1 round-2 findings file is absent; its complete review was available in the corresponding stdout log.\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/admission.